%%
%% The abstract is a short summary of the work to be presented in the
%% article.

\begin{abstract}
% 1. RE in a nutshell and how it is solved by LLMs
Relation extraction (RE) is the automatic identification of semantic relationships between named entities in text. Large language models (LLMs) have recently made RE accessible through zero- and few-shot prompting, with strong performance, without the need for task-specific fine-tuning.
% 2a. The multilingual challenge: valuable because annotated data is scarce, yet harder in non-English settings
LLM-based RE is particularly valuable in multilingual settings, where annotated training data is scarce for most language pairs, yet unlike in English, performance often degrades in non-English and lower-resource settings, even for models with broad multilingual coverage.
% 2b. The historical/OCR/archaic challenge: harder regardless
These difficulties are further compounded in historical document archives, where OCR digitisation introduces systematic character-level noise, 19th-century spelling and syntax diverge sharply from modern pre-training distributions, and domain-specific adaptation is rarely feasible.
% 3. We explored this setting on multiple languages and corpora, as part of the HIPE evaluation lab at CLEF 2026
We investigate this challenging intersection across historical newspaper corpora in German, English, and French, qualifying annotated person--place pairs along two relations, \texttt{at} (was the person at the location?) and \texttt{isAt} (is it their current habitual location?), as part of the HIPE 2026 evaluation lab at CLEF 2026.
% 4. Our methodology: multilingual prompt engineering
Our methodology centers on carefully engineered multilingual prompts: we design a unified cross-lingual prompting framework, with explicit relation definitions, chain-of-thought reasoning, and OCR-robustness rules, and evaluate it systematically across all three document languages with no task-specific fine-tuning. A central design question we address empirically is whether prompting in a high-resource language (English) or in the native source language (German, French) better serves performance across models and language pairs.
% 5. Results and strong conclusions
All in all, our best submission ranks \textbf{9th out of 46} for French and \textbf{20th overall}; on the out-of-domain surprise set, our most generalisable model ranks \textbf{3rd out of 44} in binary evaluation, demonstrating that careful prompt engineering alone yields competitive, cross-lingual, and generalisable performance.
% 6. Call for action
We present an exploratory methodology for multilingual historical RE that generalises across languages, domains, and model families, and can serve as a competitive zero-shot baseline for broader relation extraction challenges in a multilingual environment.
\end{abstract}

