\section{Introduction}

% 1. RE in a nutshell and how it is solved by LLMs
Relation extraction (RE) is the task of automatically identifying and classifying semantic relationships between named entities in unstructured text, underpinning knowledge graph construction, database population, and downstream applications such as question answering and fact verification.
Classical RE systems required large task-specific annotated datasets, making them expensive to extend to new domains or languages.
The emergence of large language models (LLMs) has transformed this picture: through in-context learning, modern LLMs can perform competitive RE without updating any model weights, enabling rapid, annotation-free deployment across new settings.

% 2a. The multilingual challenge: valuable because annotated data is scarce, yet harder in non-English settings
This zero-shot capability is especially valuable in multilingual settings: decades of NLP progress have been concentrated on English, leaving most other languages without the supervised benchmarks needed to train dedicated RE systems \citep{joshi-etal-2020-state}, and even broadly multilingual LLMs exhibit performance drops outside English, sensitive to linguistic distance and pre-training coverage.

% 2b. The historical/OCR/archaic challenge: harder regardless
These difficulties compound further in historical newspaper archives, where OCR digitisation introduces systematic noise and archaic spelling conventions diverge from modern pre-training distributions --- challenges detailed in Section~\ref{sec:challenge} --- making zero-shot prompting especially attractive as a methodology.

% 3. We investigated this setting on different data, as part of the HIPE evaluation lab at CLEF 2026
We explore this intersection in a systematic study targeting person--place relation qualification in historical newspaper corpora across German, English, and French.
The task requires classifying each annotated pair along two semantically subtle, class-imbalanced relations, \texttt{at} (was the person at the location?) and \texttt{isAt} (is it their habitual place?), demanding multi-step inference rather than surface matching.
This investigation is conducted as part of the \textbf{HIPE 2026 evaluation lab} at CLEF 2026, a competitive shared task with 17 participating teams and 46--47 submissions per language, evaluated by global macro-recall \citep{opitz_extended_2026, opitz_overview_2026}.

% 4. Our methodology
At the core of our approach is a carefully engineered multilingual prompting framework operating across all three document languages with no task-specific fine-tuning, incorporating explicit relation definitions, chain-of-thought reasoning, OCR-robustness rules, and structured JSON output.
A key empirical question is the effect of \emph{prompt language}: prompting in a high-resource language (English) versus the native document language (German, French).
We systematically evaluate this alongside shot count (0, 3, 5, and 10 in-context examples) across all four models, selecting the best configuration per model and language on the development set.

% 5. Results and strong conclusions
Our submissions demonstrate that carefully crafted zero-shot LLM prompting is a strong strategy: our best run ranks \textbf{9th out of 46} for French and \textbf{20th overall}, and on the out-of-domain surprise test set our most generalisable model ranks \textbf{3rd out of 44} in binary evaluation, strong on French and out-of-domain generalisation, mid-table on aggregate.
We further find that English-language prompts generalise well across all document languages, that few-shot examples benefit sparse Mixture-of-Experts architectures but can degrade denser models, and that development rankings do not always transfer to the test set.

% 6. Contributions and reproducibility
Our primary contribution is a systematic empirical investigation of multilingual LLM-based RE under historical OCR conditions; the following enumerate the specific components and findings it yields.

\noindent\textbf{Our contributions are:}
\begin{enumerate}[noitemsep]
  \item A cross-lingual prompting framework for historical person--place RE incorporating explicit relation definitions, OCR-robustness rules, chain-of-thought output, and structured JSON --- serving as the controlled experimental vehicle for contributions 2--4 and as a reproducible baseline for the community.
  \item A systematic empirical study of prompt language (English vs.\ native) and shot count (0--10) across four open-weight models and three languages, yielding actionable insights on when native-language prompts help and when English generalises better.
  \item A qualitative error analysis identifying four recurring error types: PROBABLE/TRUE over-prediction, origin-location confusion, OCR-induced parse failures, and cross-entity context bleed.
  \item A fully reproducible baseline: all code, prompts, and result files are publicly released at \url{https://github.com/Ronke21/HIPE-2026-Evaluating-Person-Place-Relation-Extraction}, addressing the reproducibility gap in multilingual RE research \citep{ali2025multilingual}.
\end{enumerate}

Sections~\ref{sec:challenge}--\ref{sec:task} describe the challenge and task; Section~\ref{sec:method} covers data and experimental setup; Section~\ref{sec:results} reports results; Section~\ref{sec:analysis} discusses findings and error analysis; Section~\ref{sec:related} covers related work and future directions; Section~\ref{sec:conclusions} concludes.


\section{The Challenge}\label{sec:challenge}

Multilingual LLMs underperform on RE outside English, with the gap widening for morphologically complex languages such as German and for domains underrepresented in pre-training \citep{joshi-etal-2020-state,keinan2025emotional}.

These challenges compound sharply in the historical newspaper domain.
The HIPE 2026 corpus is digitised from microfilm, introducing OCR errors that corrupt the tokens a model needs to reason over.
Common artefacts in the corpus include character substitutions arising from Gothic typefaces, the letter `M' frequently rendered as `rn', the historical long-s glyph misread as `f', hyphenation at line boundaries that splits proper names across lines, and corrupted entity mentions.
A representative sentence from the corpus might arrive as:
\begin{quote}
\small\texttt{Herr v. Rnöller, Bürgermeifter zu Müncben, ift in Ber-\\
lin eingetroffen.}
\end{quote}
\noindent where \texttt{Rnöller} reflects Gothic M\,$\to$\,rn confusion, \texttt{Bürgermeifter} the long-s\,$\to$\,f substitution, \texttt{Müncben} a ch\,$\to$\,cb substitution, and \texttt{Ber-lin} a hyphenation artefact, all compounding in a single entity-bearing sentence.
Beyond OCR, 19th-century German uses pre-reform spelling conventions (\textit{Thür} for \textit{Tür}), and French sources of the same period employ period-specific typography and abbreviations absent from modern training corpora.
Models pre-trained primarily on contemporary web text may support the languages in principle yet lack exposure to these historical registers.
More broadly, linguistic phenomena that already challenge cross-lingual RE in contemporary settings, compounding, inflection, pro-drop, and variable word order, are further compounded when OCR noise corrupts the very morphological markers a model must resolve to identify entity-bearing tokens \citep{hennig2023multitacred}.
In this sense, historical newspaper archives present the characteristics of a low-resource domain even for languages that are otherwise well-resourced: archaic register, pre-reform spelling, and OCR-degraded surface forms place the text far outside modern pre-training distributions, effectively confronting the model with an out-of-distribution language variant \citep{ali2025multilingual,pakray2025natural}.

The \texttt{at} relation qualification task adds semantic difficulty on top of these surface challenges.
Deciding whether a person was \texttt{at} a location requires inferring presence from indirect cues, a title implying a residence, a reported action entailing presence, a temporal phrase indicating a past visit, rather than simple entity co-occurrence.
The \texttt{isAt} relation is subtler still: it requires evidence of a \emph{current or habitual} connection, not merely a past mention.
Both relations are strongly class-imbalanced: \texttt{FALSE} dominates in most article--pair combinations, with minority positive classes (\texttt{at=TRUE}, \texttt{at=PROBABLE}, \texttt{isAt=TRUE}) carrying the discriminative signal.
This skew is typical of distantly supervised multilingual RE \citep{cabot2023redfm,zhang-etal-2019-long,mintz-etal-2009-distant}; the all-\texttt{FALSE} baseline and metric choice are formalised in Section~\ref{sec:task}.

Together, cross-lingual degradation, historical OCR noise, and class-imbalanced semantic subtlety define a setting where prompt engineering and in-context calibration matter as much as model choice.


\section{The HIPE 2026 Task}\label{sec:task}

\subsection{Task Definition}

At a high level, the task asks the following: given a historical newspaper article and a pre-identified (person, place) pair mentioned in it, determine two things.
First, does the article provide evidence that the person was at the location at any point before publication, and if so how strong is that evidence?
Second, was that location the person's \emph{current or habitual} place at the time of writing?
The first question has three possible answers (\texttt{TRUE}, \texttt{PROBABLE}, or \texttt{FALSE}), depending on whether the evidence is direct, indirect, or absent.
The second is binary (\texttt{TRUE} or \texttt{FALSE}) and strictly stronger: \texttt{isAt=TRUE} logically entails \texttt{at=TRUE} (a habitual location requires a visit).
A system must produce both labels for every (person, place) pair in every article.
HIPE 2026 \citep{opitz_extended_2026, opitz_overview_2026} represents one of the few controlled evaluation environments for multilingual RE, providing consistent annotation protocols and a unified evaluation metric across three languages simultaneously, enabling direct cross-lingual comparison under a shared task infrastructure \citep{ehrmann2020hipe,ehrmann2022hipe}.

We formalise this as follows.

\begin{defn}[Person--Place Relation Qualification]\label{def:rq}
Let $d$ be a historical newspaper article in language $\ell \in \{\mathrm{DE},\mathrm{EN},\mathrm{FR}\}$, and let
$\mathcal{P}(d) = \{(e_p,\,e_l)\}$
be the set of pre-annotated \emph{person--place pairs} in~$d$, where $e_p$ is a person mention and $e_l$ is a location mention. The task is to learn a function
\begin{equation}\label{eq:task}
  f:\; d,\,(e_p,\,e_l)\;\mapsto\;
  \bigl(\,\hat{y}_{\texttt{at}},\;\hat{y}_{\texttt{isAt}}\,\bigr)
\end{equation}
where $\hat{y}_{\texttt{at}} \in \mathcal{Y}_{\texttt{at}} = \{\texttt{TRUE},\,\texttt{PROBABLE},\,\texttt{FALSE}\}$ and $\hat{y}_{\texttt{isAt}} \in \mathcal{Y}_{\texttt{isAt}} = \{\texttt{TRUE},\,\texttt{FALSE}\}$, subject to the \emph{consistency constraint}:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:constraint}
  \hat{y}_{\texttt{isAt}} = \texttt{TRUE}
  \;\Rightarrow\;
  \hat{y}_{\texttt{at}} = \texttt{TRUE}
\end{equation}
No task-specific fine-tuning is applied; all model weights are fixed at inference time.
\end{defn}

\subsection{Evaluation Metric}

A predicted label is evaluated by \textbf{exact string equality} against the gold annotation: the evaluation script compares the predicted label string (\texttt{"TRUE"}, \texttt{"PROBABLE"}, or \texttt{"FALSE"}) to the gold string character-by-character, with no fuzzy matching.
This is important to note: OCR noise affects the entity mentions $(e_p, e_l)$ that are given as input, but the evaluation operates only on the output label strings, which are controlled vocabulary.
The gold labels were produced by human annotators who reviewed and corrected LLM-generated pre-annotations, and are treated as ground truth.

The primary metric is \textbf{global macro-recall} (GMR), computed as a two-level average: first, per-relation macro-recall (uniform average over that relation's label classes); then a uniform average across the two relations:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gmr}
  \mathrm{GMR}
  = \tfrac{1}{2}\!\left[
    \frac{1}{|\mathcal{Y}_{\texttt{at}}|}\sum_{c\,\in\,\mathcal{Y}_{\texttt{at}}}\!\mathrm{Recall}(c)
    \;+\;
    \frac{1}{|\mathcal{Y}_{\texttt{isAt}}|}\sum_{c\,\in\,\mathcal{Y}_{\texttt{isAt}}}\!\mathrm{Recall}(c)
  \right]
\end{equation}
Each of the three \texttt{at} classes and each of the two \texttt{isAt} classes contributes equally to the score.
Macro-recall is used rather than accuracy or F1 because it equally penalises missing any class, whether a rare positive (\texttt{at-TRUE}, \texttt{at-PROBABLE}, \texttt{isAt-TRUE}) or the dominant \texttt{FALSE}, discouraging systems from exploiting the class imbalance.
A system predicting all pairs as \texttt{FALSE} achieves $\mathrm{GMR} = \tfrac{1}{2}\!\left(\tfrac{1}{3} + \tfrac{1}{2}\right) = \tfrac{5}{12} \approx \mathbf{0.4167}$ --- the dominant-class baseline.

A secondary \textbf{binary evaluation} maps $\hat{y}_{\texttt{at}} = \texttt{PROBABLE} \to \texttt{TRUE}$, reducing $\mathcal{Y}_{\texttt{at}}$ to two classes (\texttt{TRUE}/\texttt{FALSE}).
This pools all evidence of presence, whether the model was certain (\texttt{TRUE}) or uncertain (\texttt{PROBABLE}), into a single positive class, giving an upper-bound view of how well a system identifies any spatial connection at all.


\section{Experimental Design}\label{sec:method}

\subsection{The Data}

The data was provided by the Impresso project (\url{https://impresso-project.ch}), which digitized and semantically enriched historical European newspaper archives. The HIPE 2026 corpus extends the HIPE-2022 v2.1 named entity-annotated collection, covering newspapers in German, English, and French from roughly the 1780s to the 1950s. Named entity annotations include surface mention offsets, entity type (person, location), and Wikidata~\citep{vrandevcic2014wikidata} QID links where available. Person--place pairs were first pre-annotated by an LLM ensemble, then manually reviewed and corrected by domain experts.

Table~\ref{tab:data} shows the number of articles and annotated person--place pairs per language and split. The French portion of the training set is substantially larger than German and English combined, reflecting the composition of the underlying newspaper archives.

